// Mastery check: HTML and CSS (practice, ungraded).
// One scenario set across the module's topics. Titles name the topic and
// objective id each item practises. About 15-20 minutes.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/mastery-check

::semantic-html-page-structure o1 picking the element::The Trail Guides page gains a weather box that stands on its own next to the trail content, with its own small heading. Which element fits the box best? {
=aside #Correct. Content related to the page but standing apart from the main flow is exactly what aside describes.
~main #Not quite. A page has one main, and it already holds the trail content itself.
~footer #Not quite. The footer carries end-of-page material such as small print, not a side box.
~span #Not quite. span is a generic inline wrapper with no meaning at all.
}

::semantic-html-page-structure o2 the next level down::A page outline reads h1 "Trail Guides", then h2 "Pick a trail" and h2 "Join a guided hike". Inside "Pick a trail", each trail card needs its own heading. Which level do the cards use? {
=h3, because a heading one level below its h2 section keeps the outline unbroken #Correct. Levels step down one at a time from the heading above.
~h2, so the cards match the section headings #Not quite. The cards sit inside an h2 section, so they need the next level down, not a sibling level.
~h4, to make the cards visibly smaller #Not quite. That skips h3; size is styled with CSS, never by picking a lower level.
~h1, because every important block starts a fresh outline #Not quite. One h1 names the page; everything else nests below it.
}

::semantic-html-page-structure o3 swapping the wrapper::A review finds the trail links wrapped in a div with class "menu". You replace the div with a more meaningful element and change nothing else. Which element announces the links' purpose while leaving the layout as it was? {
=nav #Correct. nav marks a block of navigation links, and like div it is a plain block-level box, so the layout stays the same.
~button #Not quite. A button is a control that triggers an action, not a wrapper for a set of links, and it changes rendering.
~table #Not quite. Tables are for two-dimensional data and bring their own layout behavior.
~strong #Not quite. strong marks important text inline; it neither groups links nor keeps block layout.
}

::forms-and-html5-validation o3 checking twice::The sign-up form blocks an empty name and a malformed email in the browser. A teammate asks why the API that receives the sign-up validates the same rules again. What is the reason? {
=A request can be sent straight to the API without the form, so the browser checks are skippable #Correct. Anyone can craft a request with other tools; only server-side checks guard the data every time.
~The browser checks are too slow for large forms #Not quite. Built-in validation is immediate; speed is not the concern.
~HTML validation only works while the page is online #Not quite. Built-in validation runs entirely in the browser, online or not.
~The server cannot read requests that failed browser validation #Not quite. The server never hears about blocked submissions; the issue is requests that bypass the form entirely.
}

::accessibility-basics o2 a button in review::A pull request adds a clickable div with a button role, a tabindex and a click script to submit the sign-up form. The behavior works with a mouse. What should the review ask for, and why? {
=Use a button element: it is focusable, keyboard-operable and announced correctly without any of those extras #Correct. When a native element gives the behavior, it replaces the role, the tabindex and part of the script at once.
~Keep the div but add more ARIA attributes until screen readers accept it #Not quite. ARIA can name the div a button, but Space and Enter handling still has to be rebuilt by hand.
~Keep the div because button elements cannot be styled to match the design #Not quite. Buttons are fully styleable with CSS.
~Replace the div with a link, since links are focusable too #Not quite. A link navigates; submitting a form is a button's job.
}

::the-box-model o1 naming the gap::In the card design, the trail name sits on a green background, then there is breathing room inside the card's border, then empty space separates neighbouring cards. The breathing room inside the border is which box-model area? {
=Padding #Correct. Padding lies between the content and the border, inside the background.
~Margin #Not quite. Margin is the space outside the border that separates neighbours.
~Content #Not quite. The content area holds the text itself, not the room around it.
~Border #Not quite. The border is the drawn edge between padding and margin.
}

::the-box-model o2 fitting the column::A trail card sets width 300px, padding 20px on each side and a 2px border all round. The design column is exactly 300px wide. Which box-sizing makes the rendered card fit it? {
=border-box, because the declared 300px then includes the padding and border #Correct. Under border-box the rendered width is the declared width; the content shrinks to 256px.
~content-box, because it is the browser default #Not quite. Under content-box the card renders 300 + 40 + 4 \= 344px wide and overflows the column.
~Either one, since width always means the rendered width #Not quite. Under content-box, width sets only the content area, so the two models render differently.
~Neither; the card must declare width 256px instead #Not quite. That works arithmetic around content-box, but border-box makes the declared width fit directly.
}

::the-box-model o3 the space between cards::Two trail cards are stacked as normal block elements. The upper card has margin-bottom 24px and the lower card margin-top 16px. How much space separates them? {
=24px, because adjacent vertical margins collapse to the larger one #Correct. Stacked block margins collapse; 24 beats 16.
~40px, because the two margins add together #Not quite. Vertical margins between stacked blocks collapse instead of adding.
~16px, because the lower element wins #Not quite. The collapsed margin is the larger of the two.
~8px, the difference between them #Not quite. Collapsing keeps the larger margin; nothing is subtracted.
}

::flexbox o3 sharing the row::A 600px flex row with no padding, border or gap holds two items, each with flex-basis 100px and no margins or size limits. The first has flex-grow 1, the second flex-grow 3. How wide does the second item render? {
=400px #Correct. Leftover is 600 - 200 \= 400px; the second item takes three quarters, 300px, plus its 100px basis.
~450px #Not quite. That hands the item three quarters of the whole container, but grow shares only the leftover space.
~300px #Not quite. That is the item's share of the leftover without its own 100px basis.
~200px #Not quite. That would be an equal split, ignoring the 3 to 1 grow ratio.
}

::grid o3 rows and columns together::The photo gallery must align trail photos in strict rows and columns at once, while the nav bar lays links out along one line that wraps when narrow. Which tools fit, and why? {
=Grid for the gallery, Flexbox for the nav: Grid controls both axes, Flexbox controls one #Correct. Two-dimensional alignment is Grid's job; a one-direction row of links is Flexbox's.
~Flexbox for both, because wrapping rows behave like a grid #Not quite. Wrapped flex rows size independently, so columns drift out of alignment.
~Grid for both, because Grid is the newer tool #Not quite. Grid works for the nav, but a one-axis line of links is simpler and clearer as a flex row; age is no reason.
~Flexbox for the gallery, Grid for the nav, to balance the page #Not quite. That reverses each tool's strength.
}
